{"author":{"id":"337a442880d7106d509ff84f2f7fef51acce8bce88b081d4f4c3f0298b7e7941"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791660174,"nonce":"3mCuuyiIw2/AZOcAUHftjXm98C0=","title":"Containers: single-writer run lock shared by host and containers","message":"Spec 018 section SQLite store (ticket C5). Today nothing stops two jobhunter run (or score --collect-pending, --rescore-pending) processes at once; with host timers and container timers both enabled that could double-submit paid batches. Take an exclusive fcntl lock on \u003cdata_dir\u003e/run.lock for run, score submit, collect and rescore-pending; a second invocation exits 0 with a clear already-running message and spends nothing; the lock file records pid, hostname and command as advisory metadata only. fcntl locks work across containers sharing a bind mount on one kernel. Checks without podman: pytest with two processes on a tmp data dir showing the second does not submit (mocked scorer client counts calls) and that the lock is released when the holder dies; ruff and pytest. Needs host or CI: a C8 smoke step running two containers against one volume with a stub command, one must refuse.","files":null}]}